    <code code="51848-0" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation note"/>
    <title>Assessment</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item>Right-hemisphere lacunar infarct, recovering (I69.354). A small-vessel lacunar infarct: a single small deep infarct with a classic motor syndrome, chronic small-vessel disease in both hemispheres, no large-artery stenosis, and no cardiac source on echocardiography. Hypertension is the main driver. No further vascular imaging is needed. Lacunar stroke carries a low yield for atrial fibrillation, but at 78 with left ventricular hypertrophy and earlier cerebrovascular events, 30 days of monitoring is reasonable because finding it would change treatment to anticoagulation. [S]</item><item>Essential hypertension (I10). 138/80 mmHg today, above the 130/80 target; the principal treatable risk factor. [S]</item><item>Post-stroke emotionalism with mild depressive symptoms. The sudden tearfulness out of proportion to his feelings is post-stroke emotionalism. He also has mild depressive symptoms (PHQ-9 8, no suicidal thoughts) that are improving as function returns. He does not meet criteria for a depressive disorder, and treatment is not needed now. [S]</item></list><paragraph>Data reviewed: Hospital MRI and CT angiography, transthoracic echocardiogram, 72 hours of inpatient telemetry, the ECG and laboratory results from the first primary care visit, the PHQ-9 and MoCA scores, and the referral letter. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
</component>

<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.10" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="18776-5" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Plan of care note"/>
    <title>Plan of Treatment</title>
    <text><list><item>Aspirin has been stopped after 21 days; continue clopidogrel 75 mg daily long term. [S]</item><item>Continue atorvastatin 80 mg; lipid panel 4 to 12 weeks after starting, aiming for LDL cholesterol below 70 mg/dL. [S]</item><item>Lower blood pressure below 130/80 mmHg; I agree with adding lisinopril, with standing pressures checked because of his age. [S]</item><item>30-day ambulatory ECG monitoring, fitted today; if atrial fibrillation is found, change to an oral anticoagulant. [S]</item><item>No further vascular imaging; repeat brain imaging only for new symptoms. [S]</item><item>Continue physical and occupational therapy. Return to work gradually: half days from about six weeks, full days and major speeches by about three months, with rest before long speeches. Air travel is reasonable once he walks confidently outdoors. [S]</item><item>Repeat the PHQ-9 at each primary care review; if it reaches 10 or more, or if the tearfulness becomes distressing, consider an SSRI. [S]</item><item>Stop smoking cigars, and keep alcohol to no more than two drinks a day. [S]</item><item>Repeat the MoCA within the first year. [S]</item><item>Discussed with Dr. Okafor by telephone today; the patient agrees with the plan. [S]</item><item>Follow-up: Primary care will manage monitoring on the CMID stroke schedule and send the monitor result. Neurology will see him again for any new neurological symptom, atrial fibrillation on monitoring, or a PHQ-9 of 10 or more. [S]</item></list></text>

  </section>
